README

This directory contains the Circle port of SOEM (Simple Open EtherCAT Master),
which allows to use a Raspberry Pi as EtherCAT master in a bare metal
application. EtherCAT frames are sent to and received from a Circle net device
(CNetDevice) directly, without a TCP/IP stack in between.

SOEM is developed by the OpenEtherCATsociety and is not part of Circle. It is
dual-licensed under GPLv3 and a commercial license. Please see the file
LICENSE.md in the SOEM sources for the full license information.

Only the OS abstraction layer (osal), the hardware abstraction layer (oshw)
and the NIC driver (nicdrv) of SOEM have been ported here. The sources of the
SOEM library itself are not included in Circle and have to be downloaded
before this library can be built.


GET THE SOEM SOURCES

Change to this directory (addon/soem) and enter:

	git clone https://github.com/OpenEtherCATsociety/SOEM.git

This port has been developed for SOEM 2.x, which provides the ecx_* API with
an explicit context (ecx_contextt). The build system of SOEM (CMake) is not
used. The build options, which are normally generated by CMake into the file
include/soem/ec_options.h, are defined statically in the file
include/soem/ec_options.h in this directory instead.


BUILD

Build the Circle libraries first (see the Circle README), then enter here:

	make

This creates the library libsoem.a, which contains the SOEM library and its
Circle port. If the SOEM sources have been cloned to another directory, the
variable SOEMHOME can be set, for example:

	make SOEMHOME=/path/to/SOEM

A demonstration of this library is in sample/47-ethercatweb.


USING THE LIBRARY

Add these files to the LIBS variable of your application Makefile:

	$(CIRCLEHOME)/addon/soem/libsoem.a
	$(CIRCLEHOME)/lib/sched/libsched.a

and these include paths (SOEMHOME is addon/soem/SOEM by default):

	INCLUDE += -I $(CIRCLEHOME)/addon/soem \
		   -I $(CIRCLEHOME)/addon/soem/include \
		   -I $(SOEMHOME)/include -I $(SOEMHOME)/osal

C++ files must include <soemcpp.h> instead of <soem/soem.h>. SOEM defines the
type "boolean" as uint8_t, while Circle defines it as bool. The header file
soemcpp.h renames the SOEM type to "ec_boolean", so that Circle classes and
the SOEM API can be used in the same file.

The EtherCAT master is initialized with the name of the net device to be used
("eth0", "eth1", ...), which is the zero-based index of the net device within
the Ethernet devices, known to Circle:

	ecx_contextt *pContext = new ecx_contextt;
	memset (pContext, 0, sizeof *pContext);		// must be zeroed!

	if (ecx_init (pContext, "eth0"))
	{
		...
	}

The net device must not be used by a CNetSubSystem instance at the same time.
See doc/multiple-net-devices.txt for the numbering of the net devices, if your
application uses two Ethernet ports (one for EtherCAT, one for TCP/IP).

Because the net device is not managed by a TCP/IP stack, the application has
to call ecx_updatephy() about every two seconds, so that the settings of the
net device are updated according to the PHY status (link speed and duplex
mode). ecx_linkup() returns, if the link is up.


REQUIREMENTS AND LIMITATIONS

* The Circle scheduler (lib/sched) is required. SOEM must be called from a
  task, not from an interrupt handler.
* The scheduler of Circle is cooperative and non-preemptive. A task, which
  waits for an EtherCAT frame to return, does not give control to other tasks
  in the meantime. Cycle times below one millisecond are not recommended.
* Line redundancy (two net devices) is not supported. ecx_init_redundant()
  fails.
* Distributed clocks (DC) are supported, but there is no synchronization of
  the system time of the Raspberry Pi to the DC master clock.
* The EEPROM tools and the file access over EtherCAT (FoE) firmware update
  functions of SOEM are not tested with this port.
